ls -l - Lists files and directories in long format, showing detailed information for each: permissions, number of links, owner, group, file size, and last modified date/time. It's the primary command used to inspect current permission and ownership settings before making any changes.
chmod - Short for "change mode". Modifies the read(r), write(w), and execute(x) permissions of a file or directory, for three categories of user: the owner, the group, and others. Can be used with symbolic notation (e.g chmod u+x file) or numeric/octal notation (e.g. chmod 755 file).
useradd - Creates a new user account on the system. on its own it only creates a minimal account entry in /etc/passwd; common flags extend this: -m creates a home directory, -s sets the login shell, and -G adds the user to supplementary groups. Requires root/sudo privileges to run.
